## 7. Open decisions — FLAGGED for the Architect's ratify, NOT resolved here

### [DD-BPR-MOCK-CAN] Which mock/virtual CAN interface backs Slice B testing
- decision: OPEN — not selected.
- status: open
- rationale: Slice B must be testable before physical CAN hardware exists; the mock choice determines the harness shape, fidelity of acceptance-mask emulation, and CI portability. Deferred to the Architect because it trades CI simplicity against hardware-path fidelity.
- alternatives: (a) Linux `vcan` (SocketCAN virtual interface) — high fidelity, Linux-only, needs kernel module in CI; (b) a pure-Python in-memory CAN mock modeling arbitration IDs + acceptance masks — portable, lower fidelity; (c) `python-can` with its virtual bus backend — middle ground, adds a dependency.
- invalidating_assumption: that CI runs on Linux with SocketCAN available. If CI must stay OS-agnostic or run in restricted containers, (a) is off the table and (b)/(c) win.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PEER]

### [DD-BPR-RELAY-WRAP] Does the multiplexed channel REPLACE coordination_relay outright or WRAP it
- decision: OPEN — not selected.
- status: open
- rationale: `coordination_relay` (`:8422`, `transport_http_relay.py`) is one of the four ports [BPR-ONE-CHANNEL] collapses. Whether to retire it and route Architect/Builder coordination through the peer channel, or keep it and wrap it as a multiplexed substream, affects migration risk and existing Haven-coordination consumers.
- status-note: this spec mandates the ONE-channel contract; it does not mandate the disposition of the existing relay. [BPR-TRANSPORT-CONTRACT] narrows the framing: `coordination_relay`'s coordination role is a bridge-configured peer and its telemetry role is an egress-configured thin adaptor — so the disposition is per-ROLE, not per-process, which is new context for this decision.
- alternatives: (a) REPLACE — coordination rides the peer channel as a substream; retire `:8422`; cleanest end-state, higher migration risk. (b) WRAP — keep `HttpRelayTransport` behind the peer as an internal substream; lower risk, carries the old surface longer.
- invalidating_assumption: that no external consumer depends on `:8422`'s HTTP shape directly. If a non-ComposeBus consumer POSTs to `:8422`, REPLACE breaks it and WRAP is forced.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PEER]

### [DD-BPR-AUTH] The exact peer-handshake / auth mechanism over Tailscale
- decision: OPEN — not selected.
- status: open
- rationale: [BPR-AUTH] fixes the invariant (auth independent of network trust, over Tailscale, ACL-scoped) but not the concrete handshake. Options differ in whether app-layer identity is derived from the tailnet or established independently.
- alternatives: (a) lean on Tailscale identity alone (ACL tag scope + WireGuard) with no app-layer handshake; (b) a mutual app-layer token/cert handshake ON TOP of Tailscale (defense-in-depth, survives a LAN-pinhole fallback where there is no tailnet identity); (c) a capability token scoped to the subscribed mirror set (auth + authorization of scope in one).
- invalidating_assumption: that the sync always rides Tailscale. If the LAN-pinhole fallback is ever used, (a) has no network identity to lean on and (b)/(c) become mandatory.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PEER]

