## 8. AMENDMENT — the ONE unified Transport-emitter contract ([DEC-BUS-PEER-UNIFIED-TRANSPORT-CONTRACT])

> Amendment authored 2026-07-25. This section REFINES and UNIFIES the peer shape of §1–§2; it introduces NO invariant that contradicts §2. It names ONE `Transport` interface with THREE declared knobs and a thin-to-fat implementation spectrum, and lands the two existing emitters on it. Resolved by design DISCUSSION, not an A/B.

### [BPR-TRANSPORT-CONTRACT] One Transport interface, three knobs, a thin-to-fat spectrum

- definition: every ComposeBus emitter — from a fire-and-forget telemetry POST to a full segment-crossing peer — is ONE `Transport` contract (`[SPEC:CIO-APP]` waist: `send`/`subscribe_all`/`start`/`stop`/`is_connected`) plus a declared `TransportProfile` of THREE orthogonal knobs. It is NOT two forked emitters and NOT one monolith: it is ONE interface whose IMPLEMENTATIONS span a spectrum — a FAT peer at the reliable/bridge end, a THIN adaptor at the cheap egress end — both lowering to the same waist.
- key-insight: the real axis is EGRESS (ComposeBus -> a NON-bus sink; one-way, best-effort; telemetry is the exemplar) vs BRIDGE (ComposeBus <-> another bus substrate — CAN, a network segment, browser-over-WS; bidirectional, reliable; the CAN bridge is the exemplar). The unification is the INTERFACE; the implementations stay honestly thin-vs-fat. Narrow-waist ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]) + cheapest-node-that-fully-owns-the-behavior applied to transport: unify the interface, but do NOT force trivial telemetry (a fire-and-forget POST) through the peer's handshake/mirror/ack machinery.
- requirement: an emitter MUST expose a `TransportProfile` declaring its three knobs, and its runtime behavior MUST match its declaration. An emitter declaring `QoS.CRITICAL_ACK` MUST honor [BPR-CRITICAL-ACK] end-to-end (propagate-or-explicit-degrade; silent-clear = 0). An emitter declaring `QoS.FIRE_AND_FORGET` MUST NOT be routed through — and MUST NOT invoke — the handshake/mirror/critical-ack machinery; a `pri: critical` message through it is POSTed best-effort like any other, opening NO ack-pending entry.
- boundary/error: the knob combination is self-validating — `QoS.CRITICAL_ACK` with `Directionality.EGRESS` or with `FarSide.DUMB_SINK` is a CONSTRUCTION error (a one-way emitter to a dumb sink has no return path to carry an ack, so it cannot honor the correctness bar). This makes a mis-declared profile a build-time fault, not a latent runtime silent-loss.
- reuse: this amendment RESTATES nothing from [AMEND:BP-ACK] or [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PROTOCOL]; the critical-ack protocol, cid/retry/`_bus/fault`, and the `BusMessage` waist are REUSED. The contract only declares WHERE on the spectrum an emitter sits.
- test (machine): assert both poles satisfy the interface (`isinstance(emitter, TransportContract)` for the thin `HttpRelayTransport` and the fat `PeerLink`/`PeerHub`/`CanBridge`); assert the BRIDGE pole holds silent-clear = 0 under a dropped hop; assert the EGRESS pole never constructs a `CriticalAckOrigin`/`CriticalAcker` and exposes none of the ack/handshake surface. (Realized: `tests/peer/test_transport_contract.py`.)
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PEER]

### [BPR-TC-KNOBS] The three knobs, enumerated

- definition: the `TransportProfile` is exactly three orthogonal dimensions:
  1. **QoS / reliability** — `CRITICAL_ACK` (propagate-or-explicit-degrade, [BPR-CRITICAL-ACK]) at the reliable pole vs `FIRE_AND_FORGET` (best-effort, no ack machinery) at the cheap pole.
  2. **directionality** — `BIDIRECTIONAL` (a bridge: ComposeBus <-> another bus substrate; reads+writes both ride the seam, [SCEN-BPR-A-BIDIR]) vs `EGRESS` (one-way: ComposeBus -> a non-bus sink).
  3. **far-side type** — `FULL_PEER` (another ComposeBus, full fidelity) / `MAPPED_DEVICE_BUS` (CAN — semantics mapped or synthesized-or-degraded, [BPR-B-CANMAP]/[BPR-B-SYNTH]) / `DUMB_SINK` (a POST endpoint / telemetry collector — no bus semantics on the far side, nothing to ack).
- rationale: the knobs are the minimum needed to place any emitter on the spectrum and to make the honest thin-vs-fat distinction machine-readable rather than implicit in a class name. They are orthogonal by construction save for the one coupling the correctness bar forces (critical-ack demands a bidirectional far side that can acknowledge).
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PEER]

### [BPR-TC-SPECTRUM] The thin-to-fat implementation spectrum + emitter placement

- definition: the two poles and the emitters that realize them today:

| Pole | Knobs (QoS / directionality / far-side) | Realizing emitter(s) | Machinery |
|------|------------------------------------------|----------------------|-----------|
| EGRESS (thin) | FIRE_AND_FORGET / EGRESS / DUMB_SINK | `HttpRelayTransport` (`transport_http_relay.py`) | outbound POST only; NO handshake/mirror/ack |
| BRIDGE (fat), full peer | CRITICAL_ACK / BIDIRECTIONAL / FULL_PEER | `PeerLink` + `PeerHub` (`peer/link.py`, `peer/hub.py`) | handshake + subscription-scoped mirror + capture/replay + transparent critical-ack forwarding |
| BRIDGE (fat), device bus | CRITICAL_ACK / BIDIRECTIONAL / MAPPED_DEVICE_BUS | `CanBridge` (`peer/canbridge.py`) | CAN-ID mapping + ISO-TP frag + synthesize-or-degrade critical-ack from type-8 evidence |

- key-insight: the CAN bridge (Slice B, already built) is the FIRST real test of the BRIDGE pole against a non-peer far side; telemetry (the fire-and-forget POST) is the exemplar of the EGRESS pole. Both are the SAME contract read at opposite ends of the spectrum.
- boundary/error: a future `bidirectional` HTTP relay is STILL fire-and-forget on its outbound leg — it never carries critical-ack — so it stays `QoS.FIRE_AND_FORGET` regardless of directionality; it does not migrate toward the bridge pole merely by gaining an inbound path.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PEER]

### [BPR-TC-RELAY-DOUBLE-DUTY] coordination_relay's double duty dissolves onto the two poles

- definition: `coordination_relay` (`:8422`, `transport_http_relay.py`) historically served TWO roles over one HTTP shape — Architect/Builder coordination AND telemetry/monitoring. Under [BPR-TRANSPORT-CONTRACT] those roles map onto DIFFERENT poles: the coordination role is a BRIDGE-configured peer (bidirectional, reliable — it needs delivery truth), and the telemetry role is an EGRESS-configured thin adaptor (fire-and-forget onto a dumb sink).
- key-insight: the double duty was never one thing wearing two hats — it was two points on the spectrum collapsed into one process because there was only one emitter class. Naming the knobs dissolves the conflation: each role is configured, not forked.
- rationale: this is the PAYOFF of the unification and it re-frames [DD-BPR-RELAY-WRAP] — the replace-vs-wrap disposition is now per-ROLE (coordination vs telemetry), not per-process. The telemetry role trivially lowers to the existing thin `HttpRelayTransport`; only the coordination role is a candidate to ride the peer channel as a substream.
- open-decision: the concrete disposition (retire vs wrap `:8422`, and whether coordination rides the peer channel) remains [DD-BPR-RELAY-WRAP] — this amendment supplies the framing, not the resolution.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PEER]

#### [SCEN-BPR-TC-EGRESS-CRITICAL] A critical message through the egress pole is best-effort, not acked
- when: a `pri: critical` `BusMessage` (carrying `_priority: critical` + a `cid`) is sent through an `HttpRelayTransport` (the EGRESS pole)
- then: it is POSTed fire-and-forget exactly like any other message — the adaptor opens NO ack-pending entry, constructs NO `CriticalAckOrigin`/`CriticalAcker`, awaits NO return frame, and exposes no ack/handshake surface; the `send` call returns without blocking on delivery

#### [SCEN-BPR-TC-BRIDGE-SATISFIES] Both poles are the one contract
- when: the platform inspects the thin egress adaptor and the fat bridge peer through the unified contract
- then: both satisfy `TransportContract` (the same method waist + a declared `TransportProfile`); the egress profile reads `FIRE_AND_FORGET / EGRESS / DUMB_SINK` and the bridge profile reads `CRITICAL_ACK / BIDIRECTIONAL / {FULL_PEER | MAPPED_DEVICE_BUS}` — identical shape, opposite knob settings

### [BPR-TC-CHANGE] Decision record — the unified Transport contract

- change: introduced ONE `Transport`-emitter contract (`transport_contract.py`: `TransportProfile` + the three enums `QoS`/`Directionality`/`FarSide` + a runtime-checkable `TransportContract` Protocol) and landed the two existing emitters on it — `HttpRelayTransport` declares the EGRESS pole, `PeerLink`/`PeerHub`/`CanBridge` declare the BRIDGE pole. Observable behavior unchanged: the bridge pole still preserves critical-ack (silent-clear = 0), the egress pole stays fire-and-forget and is not forced through handshake/mirror/ack machinery. (Incidental fix: `HttpRelayTransport` previously failed the `Transport` ABC — it never implemented the abstract `subscribe_all` and so could not be instantiated; the adaptation completes the waist.)
- context_for_change: `coordination_relay` carried a DOUBLE DUTY (coordination + telemetry) because only one emitter class existed; and the peer/relay work (Slices A/B) risked being read as a SECOND, forked emitter. The narrow-waist credo demands ONE interface; the cheapest-node credo forbids forcing trivial telemetry through the peer's heavy machinery. Declaring three knobs satisfies both: one interface, honestly thin-vs-fat implementations.
- invalidating_assumption: that the two knobs QoS and directionality can vary fully independently. They cannot at one corner — `CRITICAL_ACK` requires a bidirectional far side that can acknowledge (a peer or a device bus with type-8 evidence), so `CRITICAL_ACK + EGRESS` and `CRITICAL_ACK + DUMB_SINK` are construction errors. If a future far side offers a one-way yet acknowledged channel (e.g. a store-and-forward broker that later confirms), this coupling would need revisiting.
- status: accepted (design DISCUSSION [DEC-BUS-PEER-UNIFIED-TRANSPORT-CONTRACT]); implementation landed on branch `build/w1-unified-transport`, pending Architect glass.
- part-of: [SPEC:CIO-BUS-PEER]

